title: The Landing at Matanzas
tags: event
view-if: war_stage = 2

= Ashore at Matanzas

With the Florida Strait secure, the American expedition turns toward Cuba.

After weighing several possible landing sites, General Sherman selects the port of Matanzas. Close enough to threaten Havana yet beyond the range of the capital's principal defenses, it offers the best chance of establishing a permanent foothold before Spain can concentrate its forces. For months, Mambí scouts have quietly surveyed beaches, roads, and Spanish positions, slipping through the blockade to deliver invaluable intelligence. Now they wait along the coast to guide the first assault wave through the reefs and onto the beaches.

- @Land: Order the landing.
- @Withdraw: Call off the invasion.

@Land
subtitle: Force a landing and push toward Havana.
on-arrival: {!

    let american_strength = Q.invasion_force_strength + (Q.navy_strength * 0.1);

    if (american_strength > Q.spain_coastal_defense_strength) {
        Q.matanzas_outcome = "victory";
        Q.war_stage += 1;
        Q.invasion_force_strength *= 0.9;
        Q.radical_strength += 2;
        Q.middle_class_rep += 1;
    } else {
        Q.matanzas_outcome = "defeat";
        Q.invasion_force_strength *= 0.5;
        Q.conservative_dissent += 3;
        Q.middle_class_dem += 2;
    }

!}
go-to: Victory if matanzas_outcome = "victory"; lose if matanzas_outcome = "defeat"

@Withdraw
subtitle: Abandon the invasion before it lands.
on-arrival: spain_war = 0
go-to: Defeat

The invasion is called off before the first boats leave their transports. With no secure landing possible, the expedition returns to Florida. The blockade remains in place, but the opportunity to strike directly at western Cuba slips away. Spanish authorities proclaim the withdrawal as proof that the United States lacks the resolve to carry the war onto Cuban soil.

@lose
title: Defeat at Matanzas
subtitle: Oops
on-arrival: spain_war = 0; invasion_force_strength *= 0.25
go-to: Defeat

The landing collapses almost as soon as it begins. Spanish artillery catches the invasion force while it is still struggling ashore, sinking landing craft and scattering entire companies before they can form. The surviving troops are forced back to their transports under heavy fire, abandoning equipment and hundreds of casualties on the beaches.

The expedition withdraws to Florida in disorder. Any hope of a quick campaign against Havana has vanished, and Spain emerges from the battle with renewed confidence.

@Victory

= Victory at Matanzas

Before dawn, the first landing craft emerge from the morning mist under the protection of the Atlantic Squadron. Heavy naval gunfire pounds Spanish batteries overlooking the beaches as American infantry wades ashore through the surf, rifles held above their heads. Waiting beyond the shoreline, Mambí guides lead the advancing columns inland, steering them around marshes and hidden Spanish positions while engineers race to unload artillery, ammunition, and supplies.

Caught off balance by the speed of the assault, the Spanish garrison fights stubbornly but cannot prevent the Americans from securing the harbor. By nightfall, Matanzas is firmly in American hands, and fresh troops continue pouring ashore without interruption. For the first time in the war, the United States possesses a permanent foothold on Cuban soil. Supply depots are established, siege guns are landed, and the road to Havana lies open.

- @Continue: Continue

@Continue

With Matanzas secure, the expedition begins advancing west along the coastal road toward Havana. Spanish commanders rush reinforcements from across western Cuba, determined to make their stand before the capital. The next battle may decide not only the fate of Havana, but of Spain's empire in the Caribbean itself.